2. FUNCTION TEMPLATES
=========================================================

Definition
----------
A function template allows one function to work with multiple data types.

Syntax

template<typename T>
ReturnType functionName(parameters)
{
    // implementation
}

---------------------------------------------------------

Example

template<typename T>
T add(T a, T b)
{
    return a + b;
}

Usage

cout << add(10,20);          // int
cout << add(3.5,4.2);        // double
cout << add('A','B');        // char

Compiler automatically deduces T.

---------------------------------------------------------

Template Type Deduction

template<typename T>
void print(T value)
{
    cout << value;
}

print(10);        // T = int
print(3.14);      // T = double
print("Hello");   // T = const char*

Compiler deduces the template type from arguments.

---------------------------------------------------------

Using 'class' vs 'typename'

Both are equivalent.

template<class T>
void func(T);

template<typename T>
void func(T);

Modern C++ style generally prefers:

template<typename T>

---------------------------------------------------------

Multiple Template Parameters

template<typename T, typename U>
auto add(T a, U b)
{
    return a + b;
}

Usage

cout << add(10,5.5);

T = int
U = double

Return type = double

---------------------------------------------------------

Explicit Template Arguments

Normally compiler deduces the type.

add(10,20);

Equivalent to

add<int>(10,20);

Sometimes explicit specification is required.

Example

template<typename T>
void display(T value)
{
    cout << value;
}

display<int>(100);

---------------------------------------------------------

Function Template with Non-Type Parameter

template<typename T, int SIZE>
void printArray(T (&arr)[SIZE])
{
    for(const auto& x : arr)
        cout << x << " ";
}

Usage

int arr[] = {1,2,3,4};

printArray(arr);

Compiler deduces

T = int
SIZE = 4

---------------------------------------------------------

Templates with Default Parameters

template<typename T = int>
T multiply(T a, T b)
{
    return a * b;
}

multiply(2,3);

Compiler uses T = int.

---------------------------------------------------------

Template Function Overloading

Templates can be overloaded.

template<typename T>
void print(T value)
{
    cout << value;
}

template<typename T>
void print(T a, T b)
{
    cout << a << " " << b;
}

Calls

print(5);
print(5,10);

---------------------------------------------------------

Normal Function vs Template Function

void print(int)
{
    cout << "Normal";
}

template<typename T>
void print(T)
{
    cout << "Template";
}

Calls

print(10);      // Normal function

print(2.5);     // Template

Rule:
A non-template function is preferred over a template if both match equally well.

---------------------------------------------------------

Function Template Instantiation

template<typename T>
T square(T x)
{
    return x*x;
}

Calls

square(5);

Compiler creates

int square(int);

Call

square(2.5);

Compiler creates

double square(double);

---------------------------------------------------------

Limitations

Templates require operations used inside them to be valid.

Example

template<typename T>
T add(T a, T b)
{
    return a + b;
}

Fails if '+' is not defined for T.

---------------------------------------------------------

Common Interview Questions

Q1. What is a function template?
Answer:
A generic function that works with multiple data types.

---------------------------------------------------------

Q2. Why use templates?
Answer:
• Code reuse
• Compile-time polymorphism
• Type safety
• Eliminates duplicate code

---------------------------------------------------------

Q3. Difference between function overloading and function templates?

Function Overloading
--------------------
• Multiple implementations
• Different parameter types
• More code duplication

Function Templates
------------------
• One generic implementation
• Compiler generates required versions
• Less code duplication

---------------------------------------------------------

Q4. Can templates be overloaded?

Yes.

---------------------------------------------------------

Q5. Can normal functions and template functions coexist?

Yes.

The compiler prefers the normal function when both are equally good matches.

---------------------------------------------------------

Q6. When is a template instantiated?

Only when it is used (called or instantiated).

---------------------------------------------------------

Q7. Are templates runtime or compile-time?

Templates are a compile-time feature.

---------------------------------------------------------

Q8. Why are template implementations usually placed in header files?

Because the compiler must see the complete template definition during
instantiation. If only the declaration is visible, template instantiation
fails, leading to linker errors.

